A||||16||Homicidal||Do you guys have a LOT of Fiero problems?  Should I expect more?||||5||
Z||000000||Homicidal||04-28-2000||12:07 AM||||As you guys know, I have an 84 SE.  <P>Until this recent pressure place incident, I've never had any SERIOUS problems with my Fiero.  Just a bad ignition control module.<P>But I want to know:  is this just the beginning?  I want my car to stay in tip top shape but since I am not the biggest gearhead around I don't know where to start on preventative matienence.  And the LAST thing I want to do is take it into a mechanic and say "find something wrong with my car!"  <BR>That's just cash suicide.  I would attempt some feats myself, but I don't know where to start.  I've just changed the clutch (in the shop as I write this) and they are changing the oil and stuff.<P>But after I get it back, are there any tasks I can perform to TRY and prevent something serious happening?  The actual ENGINE is running GREAT.  Idles perfect, always starts on a dime, never dies, shudders, stalls (unless I stall it), nothing wrong.  <P>I think the only thing wrong is I have a sqeaky belt.  The belt on the right hand side of the car.  I use some candle wax on it and it stops, but eventually comes back.  Just needs some loosening which I can do.<P>But I love my car, a LOT and the last thing I need is another $400 job.  I couldn't afford it.  But I also want to just keep it running good.  Any suggestions for a lost wannabe-gearhead?<P>BTW, I WOULD learn the car myself, but right now I just don't have time since it's my last 36 days of high school and I just started a new job, etc..  This summer I plan do something big with my car.....don't know what......PONDERING V-8 kit.....||||reg||5
Z||000001||BAM-BAM||04-28-2000||12:38 AM||||  Homicidal,<BR> First off, you need to get yourself a Chilton Or a Haynes manual or both. Then stay in touch with the Forum, it's the best source of do it yourself info you can find. These guys/gals have a plethera of knowledge.<BR>If there is a task you want to tackle yourself and there will be(more and more as you go, it's a desease)there will be someone on the forum that has already done it and will be more than happy to walk you through it. Don't worry, It's usualy small things that go wrong and once they are fixed they will usually stay that way(if fixed right the first time). With the Forum on your side,<BR>owning a Fiero is a joy.<P> Be Cool,<BR> BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000002||Shiner||04-28-2000||12:40 AM||||It has been my experience that '84s are the most troublesome of all years.  It's mostly the little things that kill ya.  <P> The fact is that these are 12-16 year old AMERICAN cars.  Detroit wasn't really in it's glory days in the 80's.  <P> If you are having problems with the upkeep and repairs now, don't go anywhere NEAR a V8 conversion.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Homicidal||04-28-2000||12:42 AM||||I got a Chilton actually.<P>I realize my 84' is a bad year.  But it runs good NOW.  What can I do to KEEP it running good?<P>Or should I sell the damn thing and get out while I can and get a Honda Civic?||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||04-28-2000||12:43 AM||||Just keep in mind that all Fiero's are fairly old cars, and being such, they are more prone to having problems.  Also keep in mind that many of the posts are on the technical board because someone is having a problem, but how many people post topics like "my car is running great"??  Just like where I work.... The only people I talk to are the people that are having problems with their pc's, so I sometimes get the impression that good running computers are rare.  Keep the faith.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||DXR_DAD||04-28-2000||01:23 AM||||Also remember  "you get what you pay for" An 88GT with 3,000 miles on the meter for $20,000..........you shouldn't have any problems, ya know what I mean?<P>I know where this car is?????  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||13
Z||000006||Homicidal||04-28-2000||01:27 AM||||My main question is are there any minor preventative tips I could use?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fret Naught||04-28-2000||01:43 AM||||Don't get suicidal on us, it is a car, a mechanical object that is and will wear,  you will learn how to work on it just like the rest of us, each job you conquer will make the next one seem easier. Look at it as a challange then  a hobby finally it works its way towards obsession and slowly takes over your life. I would say the most important maintenance tip is oil change & lubrication. Also remember dirt is your enemy! once a year a good cleaning is wise. Keep your manual where you will read it the most often, the throne room is usually a good place. I just thumbed through my Chiltons and there is a whole chart on preventive maintenance What and when. Above all it must be fun, otherwise what is the point?  Note: I have owned a few Honda cars Fieros are much more straight forward and less complicated to work on. Plus more fun to drive.  Relax and enjoy||||reg||6
Z||000008||Homicidal||04-28-2000||01:52 AM||||So true man, so true!<P>I DO realize a car is a mechanical peice of equipment.  I had an 85' Audio 5000 Turbo (P.O.S. model  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<P>That thing ran like crap.  I got my Fiero and it ran SO good.  NOTHING wrong, or at least nothing serious at all.  Until this clutch thing.  It's a bit of a rude awaking I guess.<P>I'm just afraid my transmission will drop out and I'll be stuck.  But will be will be.  I am hoping things will usually get WORSE before going out that way I have time to fix it.<P>But one thing, you say Fiero's are more straightfoward:  is that why every mechanic wanted to charge me $300 for clutch installation because they say it's one of the hardest engines to work on?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fret Naught||04-28-2000||02:01 AM||||What I meant by strait forward was not a lot of over head cam, 3 or more valves per cylinder, less to go wrong. I have changed a few clutches in Honda cars, needed a special tool for every thing right down to the ball joint wrench. I must admit, I have not done a Fiero clutch, but the whole shooting match drops out the bottom Just like my Aerostar van I did that one last year. I didn't think I could but with a little encouragement It happened. See your confidence just grows with each task||||reg||1
Z||000010||CUTTER||04-28-2000||02:05 AM||||I bought a 87GT 10 months ago and every single month I had a few repair bills laying on my counter from bearings, shocks, CAT, lights, to the tranny. And then one day it just like....all stoped, it stoped 3 months ago and had not made or had any problems with it since. I think I finally got almost everything fixed on it. $3000.00 worth of parts. The only thing that is going wrong is the steering coulum getting kinda loose and the starter but other then that its great. 2 problems every 3-4 months sure beats 3-4 problems every month! anyone else exsperience this?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Homicidal||04-28-2000||02:15 AM||||I've had my Fiero for 9 months now and this is the only bad problem so far!||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ozzy||04-28-2000||02:32 AM||||Hang with it guy! Er...yup, I'll say it...Fiero's are a pain in the A** sometimes (as Ozzy rolls over the floor ducking from the rocks, bullets, flame throwers etc.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )I went thru the same thing with the 88 Coupe I bought last year and was always fixing something and crying about it here on the forum but...<P>These folks DO know about everything you need, if you know one end of a wrench from the other, and they are willing to help you with VALUABLE FREE information. I can't say how much this forum has saved me in dollars. <P>I got the Coupe to a point where it has been running very well. Has a bearing noise or something I need to pay attention to but it is like a woman...pay attention or else! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> I can't live without them either! <P>I own 3 Fiero's at present. 1 is soon to be sold but I intend keeping the Coupe and GT so I'm convinced the trouble is well worth it.<P>How about you???<P>Ozzy||||reg||14
Z||000013||Homicidal||04-28-2000||02:39 AM||||Yah, and I'll save more money on fixing my car than paying $200 a month for a new one.||||reg||1
Z||000014||DJRice||04-28-2000||10:56 AM||||The most valuable preventative maintance tip you'll ever get:  <P>Change your oil and lube chassis every 3000 miles.  Its cheap and easy, and if things stay lubricated well, they will last longer.<P>If you do it yourself, it will cost $10.  If you pay someone to do it, its usually $15-$30<BR>depending on where you go.<P>Flush the cooling system every couple of years, make sure your coolant is nice and green.  If its red, its the new stuff which is expensive, but lasts longer.<P>Watch your tires for abnormal wear, and occasionally let go of the steering wheel on a nice flat straight road to see if the car will continue to steer true on its own.  If its not, seek a wheel alignment.  An out of alignment front end can eat up your tires first, and then stress the rest of the front end components causing abnormal wear on wheel bearings, tie rods, and all of the other stuff that moves down there.<P>Hope that helps a bit...As I mentioned in another post, I have had my 87 for two years now, and aside from the AC work I had done when I initally bought the car, the only work the car required was a heater core and water pump, and a brake job.  Thats all stuff you see anyway when you have a car with 142K on it.  ||||reg||1
Z||000015||triker||04-28-2000||12:11 PM||||Next time you're on the road, pay attention to the cars you see sitting on the shoulder with the hood up.  There are no guarantees no matter what you drive or how much you spend.  I've owned my 87 GT for 10 years now and haven't had any more problems with it than any other car I've owned (maybe 20-25?).  When I spun a rod bearing in my 85 with only 70 miles on it after I bought it, I must say I was dreading changing the engine even though I've done a half dozen engine swaps in the last 10 years.  As it turned out, it wasn't near as bad as I'd thought.  The point I guess I'm trying to make is, once you get to know your car, it gets easier and easier to take care of any probs that come up.||||reg||1
Z||000016||mshill||04-28-2000||12:46 PM||||What triker said.  Just this last December on the way to my parents house I saw two late model Vettes (1998-2000 models) on the side of the road with hoods up.  Those things looked brand spankin new.  I just sat down last night and figured out that I have just under $3000 in my 86SE (looks and drives nice) which includes rebuilt engine with +1 cam, new clutch, all new suspension and brakes (less calipers) including poly bushings, some nice 55 series tires on 15 inch rims, all new cooling (radiator, heater core, water pump, hoses).  The only thing that is not working on the car is the cruise control and the A/C needs servicing.<P>With all of this stuff on it new,  it should be good for another 190K miles with simple maintenance.  <P>Words of advice,  if you put in $40-$50 a month replacing things like old cooling hoses, changing oil, etc., these things will last a long long time.<P>||||reg||1
